# Apache rules shipped with the built site.
#
# This file is copied into dist/ on every build, and the deploy publishes dist/
# into releases/<timestamp>/ on the host, with a `current` symlink flipped at
# the newest one. So these rules land inside every release and apply whether a
# request arrives via the symlink or a release directory directly.
#
# It deliberately lives here rather than in the docroot .htaccess one level
# above releases/, which is hand-maintained on the host and not deployed by CI.
# Rules do work there, so anything this repo should be able to change on its own
# belongs in this file instead.
#
# One thing cannot live here: folding www and http into https://trussphp.com.
# That has to run before the docroot's own rewrite into current/, so the docroot
# file owns it. A tracked, reference-only copy is in deploy/docroot.htaccess,
# which explains the ordering trap that kept a redirect dead there for days.

# No directory listings, anywhere in the site.
#
# Belt and braces rather than a new rule. The docroot .htaccess already carries
# `Options +FollowSymLinks -Indexes` (see deploy/docroot.htaccess), and Options
# inherits into subdirectories, so the tree below it is covered today. But that
# file is hand-maintained on the host and is not deployed by CI: it can drift,
# be replaced by a panel, or be restored from a .bak that predates the line.
# This file ships inside every release, so the guarantee travels with the site.
#
# It became worth stating rather than assuming with /demo/apps/, which holds one
# application page and has no index of its own until there is a second. A
# listing there would publish the directory's contents as a side effect of a
# page not being written yet.
#
# The `-Indexes` form is required, not stylistic. A relative Options merges with
# what the parent granted; an absolute one (`Options Indexes`, `Options None`)
# REPLACES it, which here would drop the +FollowSymLinks the docroot sets, and
# the whole deploy is a symlink. Never write this line without the minus.
#
# Unguarded by <IfModule> on purpose: Options is core Apache, not a module, so a
# module guard cannot protect it. What could refuse it is AllowOverride, but the
# host demonstrably permits Options in .htaccess, because the docroot file's own
# Options line is live and the site would already be answering 500 if it were
# not.
Options -Indexes

RewriteEngine On

# The deploy plumbing was reachable from the web: /current/ served the entire
# site a second time, which split analytics by page path and offered search
# engines a duplicate of every page. Redirect it to the canonical URL instead.
#
# Matching %{THE_REQUEST} (the client's original request line) rather than the
# rewritten path is what makes this safe: any internal rewrite into current/
# performed at the docroot leaves THE_REQUEST untouched, so ordinary page views
# are unaffected and only requests that genuinely target current/ or
# releases/<ts>/ are caught.
#
# ^(.*)$ keeps the remainder of the path, because per-directory rewriting has
# already stripped the matched prefix: /current/guides/theming/ redirects to
# /guides/theming/ rather than collapsing to the home page.
#
# %{HTTP_HOST} rather than a hardcoded domain, so a preview or staging host
# redirects to itself instead of bouncing traffic to production.
RewriteCond %{THE_REQUEST} \s/+(current|releases)(/|\?|\s) [NC]
RewriteRule ^(.*)$ https://%{HTTP_HOST}/$1 [R=301,L]

# One page, one URL. /demo/index.html and /demo/ served identical bytes, and
# Search Console listed both. The canonical tag already resolved which one wins,
# so this is tidiness rather than a fix: it saves a crawler the fetch it would
# otherwise spend being told the same thing.
#
# %{THE_REQUEST} again, for a sharper reason than above. /demo/ is served by
# internally resolving index.html, so matching the rewritten path would redirect
# /demo/ to /demo/ forever. The request line only says index.html when the
# client actually asked for it.
#
# ^(.*/)?index\.html$ makes the directory optional, so /index.html folds to /
# as well. Per-directory rewriting has already stripped the prefix, so $1 is the
# directory below the site root.
RewriteCond %{THE_REQUEST} \s/+[^?\s]*index\.html[\s?] [NC]
RewriteRule ^(.*/)?index\.html$ https://%{HTTP_HOST}/$1 [R=301,L]

# Add the trailing slash to a directory URL ourselves, rather than letting
# Apache's mod_dir do it.
#
# By the time this file runs, the docroot .htaccess has already rewritten the
# request internally to current/$1, preserving whatever trailing-slash-ness the
# client sent. When the client sent none, mod_dir's DirectorySlash fires and
# builds its Location from that rewritten path, so every non-trailing-slash URL
# on the site redirected through a client-visible /current/... URL:
#
#   /roadmap -> /current/roadmap/ -> /roadmap/     (two hops, internal path leaked)
#
# The current|releases rule above only matches %{THE_REQUEST}, and the client's
# request line never said current, so it cleaned up the last hop instead of
# preventing the first. This rule runs before mod_dir gets the chance, and
# builds the redirect from $1, which per-directory rewriting has already
# stripped back to the path below the site root.
#
# Matching ^(.*[^/])$ rather than testing %{REQUEST_URI} is deliberate: whether
# REQUEST_URI shows the original or the rewritten path in per-directory context
# is exactly the ambiguity that produced this bug. The pattern is self
# contained. It cannot match the empty string, so the site root is untouched and
# cannot gain a second slash.
#
# The -d test, rather than an extension regex, is what keeps static files out:
# /favicon.svg is not a directory, so it is served rather than redirected, and a
# path segment containing a dot is still classified correctly.
RewriteCond %{REQUEST_FILENAME} -d
RewriteRule ^(.*[^/])$ https://%{HTTP_HOST}/$1/ [R=301,L,QSA]

# Response headers. Every directive here is wrapped, because an unguarded
# Header on a server without mod_headers is a 500, which would take the site
# down rather than quietly do less.
<IfModule mod_headers.c>
  # Never let a browser second-guess a Content-Type we already send correctly.
  Header always set X-Content-Type-Options "nosniff"

  # Send the full URL to ourselves, the origin only to other sites, and nothing
  # at all when leaving https. The default varies by browser.
  Header always set Referrer-Policy "strict-origin-when-cross-origin"

  # HSTS closes the gap where a first visit over http can be intercepted before
  # the 301 lands. https was only forced on 2026-08-18, so this deliberately
  # starts at five minutes: HSTS is close to irreversible, since browsers honour
  # it for the whole max-age whatever the site later says. Raise it to a year in
  # its own change once this is proven.
  #
  # No includeSubDomains, which would bind hosts this site does not control, and
  # no preload, which is effectively permanent.
  Header always set Strict-Transport-Security "max-age=300"

  # HTML carries no cache policy at all today, so browsers fall back to
  # heuristic caching derived from Last-Modified. On a site that redeploys on
  # every merge, that is how a reader sees yesterday's docs with nothing to
  # explain it. no-cache still permits a cheap 304 against Last-Modified, it
  # only forbids serving from cache without asking.
  #
  # Fingerprinted assets get the opposite treatment, in a directory-scoped
  # .htaccess written into _astro/ at build time.
  <FilesMatch "\.html$">
    Header set Cache-Control "no-cache"
  </FilesMatch>
</IfModule>

# ErrorDocument resolves its local path against the DOCROOT, not against the
# directory this file sits in. The docroot has no 404.html of its own, since the
# built page only exists inside each release, so a plain `/404.html` silently
# fails and Apache serves its own generic error page. Point it through the
# symlink instead.
ErrorDocument 404 /current/404.html
